WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:04.540
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:09.840
Assessing how similar two images are has been
a long standing problem in computer graphics.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.940
For instance, if we write a new light simulation
program, we have to compare our results against

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:19.259
the output of other algorithms and a noise-free
reference image.

00:00:19.259 --> 00:00:23.880
However, this often means that we have many
noisy images where the structure of the noise

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:25.029
is different.

00:00:25.029 --> 00:00:29.880
This leads to endless arguments on which algorithm
is favorable to the others, since who really

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:34.180
gets to decide what kind of noise is favorable
and what is not?

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:38.450
These are important and long-standing questions
that we need to find answers to.

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:43.630
In an other application, we took a photorealistic
material model and wanted to visualize other

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:46.230
materials that look similar to it.

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:51.670
However, in order to do this, we need to explain
to the computer what it means that two images

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:52.670
are similar.

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:55.260
This is what we call a similarity metric.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:59.120
Have a look at this reference image, and these
two variants of it.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.500
Which one is more similar to it, the blurred
or the warped version?

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:07.750
Well, according to most humans, warping is
considered a less intrusive operation.

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:13.070
However, some of the most ubiquitous similarity
metrics, like computing a simple per-pixel

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:15.070
difference thinks otherwise.

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:16.280
Not good.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:17.820
What about this comparison?

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:19.820
Which image is closer to the reference?

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:22.000
The noisy or the blurry one?

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:27.080
Most humans say that the noisy image is more
similar, perhaps because with enough patience,

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:31.860
one could remove all the noise pixel by pixel
and get back the reference image, but in the

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:35.030
blurry image, lots of features are permanently
lost.

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:38.420
Again, the classical error metrics think otherwise.

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:39.610
Not good.

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:44.680
And now comes the twist, if we build a database
from many of these human decisions, feed it

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:49.990
into a deep neural network, we'll find that
this network will be able to learn and predict

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:52.470
how humans see differences in images.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:54.470
This is exactly what we are looking for!

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:59.350
You can see the agreement between this new
similarity metric and these example differences.

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:03.370
However, this shows the agreement on only
three images.

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:05.409
That could easily happen by chance.

00:02:05.409 --> 00:02:10.580
So, this chart shows how different techniques
correlate with how humans see differences

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:11.580
in images.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:15.980
The higher the number, the higher the chance
that it thinks similarly to humans.

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:21.849
The ones labeled with LPIPS denote the new
proposed technique used on several different

00:02:21.849 --> 00:02:24.279
classical neural network architectures.

00:02:24.279 --> 00:02:28.939
This is really great news for all kinds of
research works that include working with images.

00:02:28.939 --> 00:02:31.749
I can't wait to start experimenting with it!

00:02:31.749 --> 00:02:36.550
The paper also contains a more elaborate discussion
on failure cases as well, so make sure to

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:37.560
have a look.

